Q: How can I achieve function overloading in C? A: <p>This may not help at all, but if you're using <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clang" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Clang</a> you can use the <em><a href="https://clang.llvm.org/docs/AttributeReference.html#overloadable" rel="nofollow noreferrer">overloadable</a></em> attribute. This works even when compiling as C.</p>
<p>Header</p>
<pre><code>extern void DecodeImageNow(CGImageRef image, CGContextRef usingContext) __attribute__((overloadable));
extern void DecodeImageNow(CGImageRef image) __attribute__((overloadable));
</code></pre>
<p>Implementation</p>
<pre><code>void __attribute__((overloadable)) DecodeImageNow(CGImageRef image, CGContextRef usingContext { ... }
void __attribute__((overloadable)) DecodeImageNow(CGImageRef image) { ... }
</code></pre>

Q: What&#39;s the best pattern for keeping custom database tables and post meta synchronized without duplicating business logic? A: <p>Your <code>LearnerService</code> already does this right, its one method with synchronous writes. Don't split it into &quot;update table, fire action, hook writes post meta,&quot; since <code>update_post_meta()</code> fires its own hooks and you risk a loop.</p>
<pre><code>class LearnerService
{
    public function updateLearner(int $id, array $data): void
    {
        $this-&gt;repository-&gt;update($id, $data);
        update_post_meta($data['post_id'], 'learner_status', $data['status']);
        do_action('myplugin_learner_updated', $id, $data); // side effects only
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>For transactions, wrap both writes in a real <code>$wpdb</code> transaction, both tables need to be InnoDB.</p>
<p>And for testability, wrap <code>update_post_meta()</code> behind a small gateway interface so <code>LearnerService</code> doesn't depend on WordPress globals directly.</p>

